Find $a_{2010}, b_{2010}, c_{2010}$.

positive_text:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Let $x, y, z$ be integers such that $x + y \sqrt{2} + z \sqrt{3} = 0$. Prove that $x = y = z = 0$.
\item Suppose $p, q, r, s$ are integers and $\frac{p + \sqrt{2} q + \sqrt{3} r}{q + \sqrt{2} r + \sqrt{3} s}$ is rational. Prove that $ps = qr$.
\end{enumerate}

negatives.text:
\begin{enumerate}
\item For all integers $s, t$, and $r$, if $s + \sqrt{2} t + \sqrt{3} r = 1$, then prove that $s = t = r = 0$.
\item For all integers $a, b, c$, and $d$, if the ratio $\frac{a + \sqrt{2} b + \sqrt{3} c}{b + \sqrt{2} c + \sqrt{3} d}$ is a rational number, then prove that $ad = bc + 1$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item For all integers $s, t$, and $r$, if $s + \sqrt{2} t + \sqrt{3} r = 0$, then prove that $s + t + r = 0$.
\item For all integers $a, b, c$, and $d$, if the ratio $\frac{a + \sqrt{2} b + \sqrt{3} c}{b + \sqrt{2} c + \sqrt{3} d}$ is a rational number, then prove that $a + d = b + c$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item For all integers $s, t$, and $r$, if $s + \sqrt{2} t + \sqrt{3} r = 0$, then prove that $s^2 + t^2 + r^2 = 0$.
\item For all integers $a, b, c$, and $d$, if the ratio $\frac{a + \sqrt{2} b + \sqrt{3} c}{b + \sqrt{2} c + \sqrt{3} d}$ is a rational number, then prove that $a^2 d = b^2 c$.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Een viertal $(2, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $2 \leq b \leq c \leq d$ is goed als er een kleurtoewijzing bestaat waarbij elk geheel getal gekleurd is met rood, blauw, groen of paars, zodanig dat
- in iedere reeks van $2$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één rood is;
- in iedere reeks van $b$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één blauw is;
- in iedere reeks van $c$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één groen is;
- in iedere reeks van $d$ opeenvolgende getallen minstens één paars is.

Geef alle goede viertallen.

negatives.text:
Een viertal $(2, b, c, d)$ van positieve gehele getallen met $2 \leq b \leq c \leq d$ is goed als er een kleurtoewijzing bestaat waarbij elk geheel getal gekleurd is met rood, blauw, groen of paars, zodat
- in iedere $2$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één rood is;
- in iedere $b$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één blauw is;
- in iedere $c$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één groen is;
- in iedere $d$ opeenvolgende getallen hoogstens één paars is.

Bepaal alle goede viertallen.

positive_text:
Let $P$ be a finite non-empty set of prime numbers, and denote $|P|$ as the number of elements in $P$. Let $m(P)$ represent the maximum number of consecutive positive integers such that each is divisible by at least one prime in $P$.
(a) Prove $|P| \le m(P)$, and that equality holds precisely when $\min(P) > |P$;
(b) Demonstrate that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

negatives.text:
Let $P$ be a finite non-empty set of primes. Define $|P|$ as the number of primes in $P$, and $m(P)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers, each divisible by at least one prime in $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \ge m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\min(P) > |P$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} + 1)$.

Let $P$ be a finite non-empty set of primes. Define $|P|$ as the number of primes in $P$, and $m(P)$ as the maximum number of consecutive positive integers, each divisible by at least one prime in $P$.
(i) Show that $|P| \le m(P)$, with equality if and only if $\max(P) > |P$;
(ii) Show that $m(P) < (|P| + 1)(2^{|P|} - 1)$.

positive_text:
Let $ABCD$ be an isosceles trapezoid with perpendicular diagonals intersecting at $O$. A line through $O$ parallel to the bases intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at points $P$ and $R$, respectively. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ across the midpoint of $[BC]$. Prove that:

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

negatives.text:
In an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals, a line through the intersection point of the diagonals and parallel to the bases intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at $P$ and $R$, respectively. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ across the midpoint of $[AD]$. Prove that:

a) $QR = AD$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

In an isosceles trapezoid $ABCD$ with perpendicular diagonals, a line through the intersection point of the diagonals and parallel to the bases intersects the legs $[BC]$ and $[AD]$ at $P$ and $R$, respectively. Let $Q$ be the reflection of $P$ across the midpoint of $[BC]$. Prove that:

a) $QR = AB$;

b) $QR \perp AD$.

positive_text:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a) Prove that for any positive integer $m$, the expression $m^2 - m + 1$ can be written in the form $n^2 + n + 1$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
\item b) Suppose $p$ is a perfect square greater than 1. Demonstrate that there are positive integers $r$ and $q$ satisfying $p^2 + p + 1 = (r^2 + r + 1)(q^2 + q + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}

negatives.text:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a) Show that $m^2 + m + 1$ is an element of the set $\{n^2 + n + 1 \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, for any positive integer $m$.
\item b) Let $p$ be a perfect square, $p > 1$. Prove that there exist positive integers $r$ and $q$ such that $p^2 + p + 1 = (r^2 + r - 1)(q^2 + q + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item a) Show that $m^2 - m - 1$ is an element of the set $\{n^2 + n + 1 \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, for any positive integer $m$.
\item b) Let $p$ be a perfect square, $p > 1$. Prove that there exist positive integers $r$ and $q$ such that $p^2 + p + 1 = (r^2 - r + 1)(q^2 + q + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{enumerate}
\item a) Show that $m^2 - m + 1$ is an element of the set $\{n^2 - n + 1 \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\}$, for any positive integer $m$.
\item b) Let $p$ be a perfect square, $p > 1$. Prove that there exist positive integers $r$ and $q$ such that $p^2 - p + 1 = (r^2 + r + 1)(q^2 + q + 1)$.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
1) $f(m+n) = f(m) + f(n) + 2mn$ for all integers $m, n$;
2) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect square for every integer $x$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) + f(1)$ is a perfect square for each integer $x$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect square for each natural number $x$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{N}$ satisfying:
i) $f(m+n) = f(m)+f(n)+2mn$, for every integers $m, n$;
ii) $f(f(x)) - f(1)$ is a perfect cube for each integer $x$.

positive_text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_y^x f(t)dt - f(y)(x-y) \le \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

negatives.text:
Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) < \frac{1}{2}(x-y)^2$, for every $x, y \in I$.

Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be an interval and $f : I \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function. Prove that the following properties are equivalent:

i) $f(x) - f(y) < |x - y|$, for every $x, y \in I$;

ii) $\int_x^y f(t)dt - f(x)(y-x) \le \frac{1}{2}(x-y)$, for every $x, y \in I$.

positive_text:
\text{An integer } n > 1 \text{ is said to be *p-periodic* if the decimal expansion of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ has a repeating cycle of length } p \text{ with no non-repeating digits before the cycle begins. For example, } \frac{1}{9} = 0.\overline{1} \text{ is 1-periodic and } \frac{1}{11} = 0.\overline{09} \text{ is 2-periodic.}
\begin{enumerate}[a)]
\item Determine all p-periodic integers } n \text{ such that the first digit in the repeating cycle of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is non-zero.}
\item Identify the largest 4-periodic prime number.
\end{enumerate}

negatives.text:
\text{An integer } n > 1 \text{ is called *p-periodic* if } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is a repeating decimal with a period of length } p, \text{ and the cycle does not begin immediately after the decimal point. For example, } \frac{1}{9} = 0.\overline{1} \text{ is 1-periodic and } \frac{1}{11} = 0.\overline{09} \text{ is 2-periodic.}
\begin{enumerate}[a)]
\item Find all p-periodic numbers } n \text{ so that the first digit of the period of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is not zero.}
\item Find the largest 4-periodic prime.
\end{enumerate}

\text{An integer } n > 1 \text{ is called *p-periodic* if } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is a repeating decimal with a period of length } p, \text{ and the cycle starts immediately after the decimal point. For instance, } \frac{1}{9} = 0.\overline{1} \text{ is 1-periodic and } \frac{1}{11} = 0.\overline{09} \text{ is 2-periodic.}
\begin{enumerate}[a)]
\item Find all p-periodic numbers } n \text{ so that the first digit of the period of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is not zero.}
\item Find the smallest 4-periodic prime.
\end{enumerate}

\text{An integer } n > 1 \text{ is called *p-periodic* if } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is a repeating decimal with a period of length } p, \text{ and the cycle starts immediately after the decimal point. For example, } \frac{1}{9} = 0.\overline{1} \text{ is 1-periodic and } \frac{1}{11} = 0.\overline{09} \text{ is 2-periodic.}
\begin{enumerate}[a)]
\item Find all p-periodic numbers } n \text{ so that the last digit of the period of } \frac{1}{n} \text{ is not zero.}
\item Find the largest 4-periodic prime.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Let $f: \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}^*$ be a strictly increasing function. Prove that:
a) there exists a decreasing sequence $(a_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of positive real numbers with $\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n = 0$ such that $a_n \le 2a_{f(n)}$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$;
b) if $(b_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is a decreasing sequence of real numbers with $\lim_{n \to \infty} b_n = 0$, then there exists a decreasing sequence $(a_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $\lim_{n \to \infty} a_n = 0$ such that $b_n \le a_n \le 2a_{f(n)}$ for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$.

\text{Let } n \ge 6 \text{ be an integer. Suppose we have } n \text{ distinct hues, and we assign one hue to each square of an } n \times n \text{ grid.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Show that, for any such assignment, there exists a sequence of knight moves from the lower-left to the upper-right corner that does not involve all hues.
\item Show that, when the number of hues is reduced to } \left\lfloor \frac{2n}{3} \right\rfloor + 2, \text{ then the result in a) holds for infinitely many } n \text{ and fails for infinitely many } n.
\end{enumerate}

negatives.text:
\text{Let } n \ge 6 \text{ be an integer. We color each square of an } n \times n \text{ board using one of } n \text{ colors.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that, for any such coloring, there exists a path of a chess knight from the bottom-left to the upper-right corner that uses all the colors.
\item Prove that, if we reduce the number of colors to } \left\lfloor \frac{2n}{3} \right\rfloor + 2, \text{ then the statement from a) is true for all } n.
\end{enumerate}

\text{Let } n \ge 6 \text{ be an integer. We color each square of an } n \times n \text{ board using one of } n \text{ colors.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that, for any such coloring, there exists a path of a chess knight from the bottom-left to the upper-right corner that does not use all the colors.
\item Prove that, if we reduce the number of colors to } \left\lfloor \frac{2n}{3} \right\rfloor + 1, \text{ then the statement from a) is true for all } n.
\end{enumerate}

\text{Let } n \ge 6 \text{ be an integer. We color each square of an } n \times n \text{ board using one of } n \text{ colors.}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Prove that, for any such coloring, there exists a path of a chess knight from the bottom-left to the upper-right corner that uses at most } n - 2 \text{ colors.}
\item Prove that, if we reduce the number of colors to } \left\lfloor \frac{2n}{3} \right\rfloor + 2, \text{ then the statement from a) is true for all } n.
\end{enumerate}

positive_text:
Let $R$ be a ring. Prove that the following conditions are logically equivalent:
1) For all $x, y \in R$, there exist odd integers $m, n$ satisfying $x^2 + y^2 = (mx + ny)^2$;
2) The ring $R$ is commutative and has characteristic 2.

negatives.text:
Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
1) For every $a, b \in A$, there exist two even integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^2 + b^2 = (pa + qb)^2$;
2) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 0$.

Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
1) For every $a, b \in A$, there exist integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^2 - b^2 = (pa + qb)^2$;
2) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 0$.

Let $A$ be a ring. Prove that the following statements are equivalent:
1) For every $a, b \in A$, there exist two odd integers $p$ and $q$ such that $a^2 + b^2 = (pa + qb)^2$;
2) The ring $A$ is commutative and $1 + 1 = 1$.

positive_text:
Determine all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(1) $f'(x) = 0$ for every integer $x$;
(2) for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$, if $f'(x) = 0$, then $f(x) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(1) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
(2) if $f(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f'(x) = 0$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(1) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}$;
(2) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 1$.

Find all differentiable functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that:
(1) $f'(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{Q}$;
(2) if $f'(x) = 0$ for some $x \in \mathbb{R}$, then $f(x) = 0$.

positive_text:
Let $\triangle ABC$ be a right triangle with $\angle B = 90^\circ$, and let $I$ be the incenter. Suppose the incircle touches $AB$, $BC$, and $AC$ at points $F$, $D$, and $E$ respectively. If the intersection of $CI$ with $EF$ is $M$, and the intersection of $DM$ with $AB$ is $N$, prove that:
a) $AI = ND$;
b) $FM = \frac{EI \cdot EM}{EC}$.

Let $A$ and $B$ be positive integers, and define $\overline{AB}$ as the number formed by appending the digits of $B$ to the digits of $A$. For example, if $A = 193$ and $B = 2016$, then $\overline{AB} = 1932016$. Show that for each of the following conditions, there exist infinitely many perfect squares of the form $\overline{AB}$:

i) $A$ and $B$ are both perfect squares;

ii) $A$ and $B$ are both perfect cubes;

iii) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;

iv) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

negatives.text:
Let $A$ and $B$ be positive integers and $\overline{AB}$ be the number formed by writing the digits of $B$ after the digits of $A$. Prove that there are infinitely many perfect cubes of the form $\overline{AB}$ in the following situations:

i) $A$ and $B$ are perfect squares;

ii) $A$ and $B$ are perfect cubes;

iii) $A$ is a perfect cube and $B$ is a perfect square;

iv) $A$ is a perfect square and $B$ is a perfect cube.

positive_text:
Let $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function satisfying $f((1 - t)x + ty) \le \max\{f(x), f(y)\}$ for all $t, x, y \in [0, 1]$, and denote $m = \inf_{x \in [0, 1]} f(x)$. Prove that:
a) the set $\{x \in [0, 1] \mid f(x) = m\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $f(0) = 0$, $\int_{0}^{1} f(x) \, dx = 0$, and $g: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a non-decreasing function, then $\int_{0}^{1} f(x)g(x) \, dx \ge 0$.

negatives.text:
Let $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function such that $f((1 - t)x + ty) \ge \min\{f(x), f(y)\}$, for all $t, x, y \in [0, 1]$, and let $m = \sup_{x \in [0, 1]} f(x)$. Show that:
a) the set $\{x \in [0, 1] \mid f(x) = m\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $f(0) = 0$, $\int_{0}^{1} f(x) \, dx = 0$, and $g: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a non-decreasing function, then $\int_{0}^{1} f(x)g(x) \, dx \ge 0$.

Let $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function satisfying $f((1 - t)x + ty) \le \max\{f(x), f(y)\}$ for all $t, x, y \in [0, 1]$, and let $m = \inf_{x \in [0, 1]} f(x)$. Show that:
a) the set $\{x \in [0, 1] \mid f(x) = m\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $f(0) = 0$, $\int_{0}^{1} f(x) \, dx = 0$, and $g: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a non-increasing function, then $\int_{0}^{1} f(x)g(x) \, dx \ge 0$.

Let $f: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ be a continuous function such that $f((1 - t)x + ty) \le \max\{f(x), f(y)\}$ for all $t, x, y \in [0, 1]$, and let $m = \inf_{x \in [0, 1]} f(x)$. Show that:
a) the set $\{x \in [0, 1] \mid f(x) = m\}$ is a closed interval;
b) if $f(0) = 0$, $\int_{0}^{1} f(x) \, dx = 0$, and $g: [0, 1] \to \mathbb{R}$ is a non-decreasing function, then $\int_{0}^{1} f(x)g(x) \, dx < 0$.

positive_text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) for all sufficiently large $n$, there exists a unique $x_n > 0$ such that $\int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = \frac{1}{n}$;
b) the sequence $(n x_n)_{n>n_0}$ converges, and find its limit.

negatives.text:
Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t)^2 \, dt = 1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

Let $f: [0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ be a continuous function. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is a large enough integer, say, $n > n_0$, then $n \int_0^{x_n} f(t) \, dt = -1$ for a unique positive real number $x_n$;
b) the sequence $(nx_n)_{n>n_0}$ is convergent and evaluate its limit.

positive_text:
Let $(b_k)_{k \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers with common difference $d$ and initial term $b_1 = a$. Define $T_k = b_1^2 + b_2^2 + \dots + b_k^2$, where $k \in \mathbb{N}^*$. Show that:
a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p$ divides $T_p$;
b) $T_5$ is not a perfect square.

negatives.text:
Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$. Prove:
a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p$ divides $S_{p-1}$;
b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$. Prove:
a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p^2$ divides $S_p$;
b) $S_5$ is not a square.

Let $(a_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be an arithmetic sequence of positive integers and define $S_n = a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \dots + a_n^2$. Prove:
a) if $p$ is a prime with $p \ge 5$, then $p$ divides $S_p$;
b) $S_5$ is a square.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ such that for all real numbers $x$ and $y$:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 + 2f(y)$;
(ii) $f(x) \le f(y)$ whenever $x \le y$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 - 2f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$.

Find all functions $f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ satisfying:
(i) $f(f(x^2) + y + f(y)) = x^2 + f(y)^2$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$;
(ii) $x \le y$ implies $f(x) \le f(y)$.

positive_text:
A positive integer $n$ is cool if it is a perfect square and there exist distinct positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ such that $a > b$, $c > d$, and $n = (a^3 - b^3) + (c^3 - d^3)$. For example, $225 = 15^2 = 7^3 - 5^3 + 2^3 - 1^3$ is cool. Prove that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

negatives.text:
We call a positive integer $n$ cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, distinct from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 + b^3 + c^3 + d^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

We define a positive integer $n$ as cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, all distinct, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 - d^3$ with $a < b$ and $c < d$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

A positive integer $n$ is cool if it is a perfect square and there exist positive integers $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$, distinct from each other, with $a > b$ and $c > d$, such that $n = a^3 - b^3 + c^3 + d^3$. Show that:

a) $2025$ is cool;

b) there are infinitely many cool numbers.

positive_text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group and $H < G$ a proper subgroup. Suppose $f, g, h : G \to G$ are endomorphisms such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ for all $x, y \in G$ with $x, y \notin H$. Prove that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

negatives.text:
Let $(G, \cdot)$ be a group with a proper subgroup $H < G$. Suppose $f, g, h$ are endomorphisms of $G$ such that $f(xy) = g(x)h(y)$ for all $x, y$ in $G$ with $x \in H$ and $y \notin H$. Prove that:

a) $g = h$;

b) if $G$ is nonabelian and $H = Z(G)$, then $f = g = h$.

positive_text:
Determine the prime numbers $p, q, r, s$, with $p \le q$ and $r \le s$, such that
(1) $p + q = r + s + 1$;
(2) $p^2 + q^2 + r^2 + s^2 = 3543$.

negatives.text:
Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d - 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3543$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3544$.

Find the prime numbers $a, b, c, d$, with $a \le b$ and $c \le d$, satisfying
(1) $a + b = c + d + 1$;
(2) $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2 = 3542$.

positive_text:
Let $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $AB + BA = A + B$. Show that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

negatives.text:
Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB - BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is even, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) \ne \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

Suppose $A, B \in \mathcal{M}_n(\mathbb{C})$ satisfy $A + B = AB + BA$. Prove that:
a) if $n$ is odd, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$;
b) if $\det(A) = \det(B)$, then $\det(AB - BA) = 0$.

positive_text:
Let $n$ be the total number of languages at KAUST. For each $1 \leq k \leq n$, define $A_{k}$ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $k$ languages, and $B_{k}$ as the set of individuals such that, for any selection of $k$ distinct languages, they can speak at least one of them. Prove:

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

negatives.text:
Let $n$ be the number of languages at KAUST. For $1 \leq k \leq n$, define $A_{k}$ as the set of individuals who can speak at least $k$ languages, and $B_{k}$ as the set of individuals who can speak all $k$ distinct languages. Prove:

a. If $2k \geq n+1$, then $A_{k} \subseteq B_{k}$;

b. If $2k \leq n+1$, then $A_{k} \supseteq B_{k}$.

positive_text:
Determine all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ such that for all real numbers $x$ and $y$,
1. $f(x+1) - f(x) \geq 1$;
2. $f(x y) - f(x) f(y) \geq 0$.

negatives.text:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \leq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x y) \geq f(x) f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x y) \leq f(x) f(y)$ for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}$.

Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ satisfying the conditions:
1. $f(x+1) \geq f(x)+1$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$;
2. $f(x^2) \geq f(x)^2$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.

positive_text:
Нека је $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{k}$ низ ненегативних реалних бројева. Кажемо да је уложив у интервал $[b, c]$ ако постоје $x_{0}, x_{1}, \ldots, x_{k} \in [b, c]$ такви да је $\left|x_{i} - x_{i-1}\right| = a_{i}$ за $i = 1, 2, \ldots, k$. Ако је $a_i \leq 1$ за све $i$, низ је нормиран. За природан број $n$, доказати:
a) сваки нормиран низ дужине $2n + 1$ је уложив у $\left[0, 2 - \frac{1}{2^n}\right]$;
б) постоји нормиран низ дужине $4n + 3$ који није уложив у $\left[0, 2 - \frac{1}{2^n}\right]$.

negatives.text:
За низ ненегативних реалних бројева $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{k}$ кажемо да је уложив у интервал $[b, c]$ ако постоје бројеви $x_{0}, x_{1}, \ldots, x_{k} \in [b, c]$ такви да важи $\left|x_{i}-x_{i-1}\right|=a_{i}$ за $i=1,2,\ldots,k$. Низ је нормиран ако су сви његови чланови не већи од 1. За природан број $n$, доказати:
a) сваки нормиран низ дужине $2n+1$ је уложив у интервал $\left[0,2+\frac{1}{2^{n}}\right]$;
б) постоји нормиран низ дужине $4n+3$ који није уложив у $\left[0,2+\frac{1}{2^{n}}\right]$.

За низ ненегативних реалних бројева $a_{1}, a_{2}, \ldots, a_{k}$ кажемо да је уложив у интервал $[b, c]$ ако постоје бројеви $x_{0}, x_{1}, \ldots, x_{k} \in [b, c]$ такви да важи $\left|x_{i}-x_{i-1}\right|=a_{i}$ за $i=1,2,\ldots,k$. Низ је нормиран ако су сви његови чланови не већи од 1. За природан број $n$, доказати:
a) сваки нормиран низ дужине $2n+1$ је уложив у интервал $\left[0,2-\frac{1}{2^{n+1}}\right]$;
б) постоји нормиран низ дужине $4n+3$ који није уложив у $\left[0,2-\frac{1}{2^{n+1}}\right]$.

positive_text:
Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space with the following properties:
(i) every pair of distinct points is connected by a single edge colored red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for any three distinct points, if the three connecting edges are not monochromatic, then they are all of different colors;
(iii) not all edges are of the same color.
Determine the largest possible value of $n$.

negatives.text:
Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every pair of distinct points is connected by a string colored red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for any three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all the same color, then they are of two different colors;
(iii) not all the strings have the same color.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every pair of distinct points is connected by a string colored red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, the three strings between them are not all of the same color;
(iii) not all the strings have the same color.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

Let $n \ge 3$ be an integer, and consider a set of $n$ points in three-dimensional space such that:
(i) every pair of distinct points is connected by a string colored red, green, blue, or yellow;
(ii) for every three distinct points, if the three strings between them are not all of the same color, then they are of two or three different colors;
(iii) not all the strings have the same color.
Find the maximum possible value of $n$.

positive_text:
Sea $f(x)$ una función continua que modela el impuesto sobre el rendimiento laboral, dependiendo del ingreso anual $x$ (en pesetas). Se conoce que:

a) $\frac{df(x)}{dx} = 0$ para $0 \leq x < 60000$;

b) $\frac{df(x)}{dx} = 1$ para $60000 < x < P$;

c) $\frac{df(x)}{dx} = 0.14$ para $x > P$;

d) $f(0) = 0$ y $f(140000) = 14000$.

Determinar el valor de $P$ y graficar $f(x)$.

negatives.text:
Sea $f(x)$ una función continua que modela un impuesto laboral dependiente del ingreso anual $x$ (en pesetas). Se conoce que:

a) $\frac{df}{dx} = 0$ para $0 \leq x < 60000$;

b) $\frac{df}{dx} = 1$ para $60000 < x < P$;

c) $\frac{df}{dx} = -0.14$ para $x > P$;

d) $f(0) = 0$ y $f(140000) = 14000$.

Calcular $P$ y graficar $f(x)$.

Sea $f(x)$ una función continua que modela un impuesto laboral dependiente del ingreso anual $x$ (en pesetas). Se conoce que:

a) $\frac{df}{dx} = 0$ para $0 \leq x < 60000$;

b) $\frac{df}{dx} = 1$ para $60000 < x < P$;

c) $\frac{df}{dx} = 0.14$ para $x > P$;

d) $f(0) = 0$ y $f(140000) = 14000$.

Calcular $P$ y graficar $f(x)$, sabiendo que $f(x)$ es una función polinómica.

positive_text:
\text{Sea } G \text{ el baricentro del triángulo } ABC. \text{ Sean } d_{a}, d_{b}, d_{c} \text{ las distancias desde } G \text{ a los lados opuestos a } A, B, C, \text{ respectivamente. Denote } r \text{ al radio de la circunferencia inscrita en } ABC.

a) \text{Demostrar que}
$$
 d_{a} \geq \frac{2r}{3}, \quad d_{b} \geq \frac{2r}{3}, \quad d_{c} \geq \frac{2r}{3}
$$

b) \text{Demostrar que}
$$
\frac{d_{a} + d_{b} + d_{c}}{r} \geq 3
$$

negatives.text:
\text{Sea } G \text{ el baricentro del triángulo } ABC. \text{ Sean } g_{a}, g_{b}, g_{c} \text{ las distancias desde } G \text{ a los lados } a, b, c, \text{ respectivamente. Sea } r \text{ el radio de la circunferencia inscrita.}

a) \text{Probar que}
$$
 g_{a} \leq \frac{2r}{3}, \quad g_{b} \leq \frac{2r}{3}, \quad g_{c} \leq \frac{2r}{3}
$$

b) \text{Probar que}
$$
\frac{g_{a}+g_{b}+g_{c}}{r} \leq 3
$$

\text{Sea } G \text{ el baricentro del triángulo } ABC. \text{ Sean } g_{a}, g_{b}, g_{c} \text{ las distancias desde } G \text{ a los lados } a, b, c, \text{ respectivamente. Sea } r \text{ el radio de la circunferencia inscrita.}

a) \text{Probar que}
$$
 g_{a} \geq \frac{2r}{3}, \quad g_{b} \geq \frac{2r}{3}, \quad g_{c} \geq \frac{2r}{3}
$$

b) \text{Probar que}
$$
\frac{g_{a}+g_{b}+g_{c}}{r} = 3
$$

\text{Sea } G \text{ el baricentro del triángulo } ABC. \text{ Sean } g_{a}, g_{b}, g_{c} \text{ las distancias desde } G \text{ a los lados } a, b, c, \text{ respectivamente. Sea } R \text{ el radio de la circunferencia circunscrita.}

a) \text{Probar que}
$$
 g_{a} \geq \frac{2R}{3}, \quad g_{b} \geq \frac{2R}{3}, \quad g_{c} \geq \frac{2R}{3}
$$

b) \text{Probar que}
$$
\frac{g_{a}+g_{b}+g_{c}}{R} \geq 3
$$

positive_text:
Für reelle Zahlen $x$, $y$, $z$, $w$ gelten die Gleichungen
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
x=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21-x}}, & y=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21-y}} \\
z=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+z}}, & w=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+w}}
\end{array}
$$
Zeige, dass $x y z w = 2004$ gilt.

negatives.text:
Für reelle Zahlen $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ gelten die Gleichungen
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
a=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+a}}, & b=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+b}} \\
c=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+c}}, & d=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+d}}
\end{array}
$$
Zeige, dass $a b c d = 2004$ gilt.

Für reelle Zahlen $a$, $b$, $c$, $d$ gelten die Gleichungen
$$
\begin{array}{ll}
a=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21-a}}, & b=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21-b}} \\
c=\sqrt{45-\sqrt{21+c}}, & d=\sqrt{45+\sqrt{21+d}}
\end{array}
$$
Zeige, dass $a + b + c + d = 2004$ gilt.

positive_text:
Show that there exists a constant $ c > 0 $ for which there are infinitely many pairs $ (n, N) $ of positive integers such that:
(a) $ N > c \cdot 2^{\omega(N)} n \log(n) $, where $ \omega(N) $ denotes the number of distinct prime factors of $ N $;
(b) for every prime factor $ p $ of $ N $ with exponent $ e $, $ n $ is divisible by $ \varphi(p^e) $, where $ \varphi $ is Euler's totient function.

negatives.text:
Prove that there is a positive real number $ c > 0 $ such that there are infinitely many pairs $ (n, N) $ of positive integers satisfying:
(a) $ N < c \cdot 2^k n \log(n) $;
(b) $ n $ is divisible by $ \varphi(p^e) $ for every prime power $ p^e $ dividing $ N $.

Prove that there is a positive real number $ c > 0 $ such that there are infinitely many pairs $ (n, N) $ of positive integers satisfying:
(a) $ N > c \cdot 2^k n \log(n) $;
(b) $ n $ is divisible by $ \varphi(p^e) + 1 $ for every prime power $ p^e $ dividing $ N $.

positive_text:
A country has $n$ cities and a company establishes a connected undirected graph of flights such that the out-degree of each vertex is equal. Flights are undirected, and at most one edge connects any two cities. The company wants to remove the minimal number of edges so that the graph becomes disconnected. Determine the minimum number of edges to remove for:
a) $n = 2008$;
b) $n = 2007$.

negatives.text:
There are $n$ cities in a country. A monopoly company establishes air traffic between some of these cities such that the graph is connected and each city has at most two flights. Flights are undirected. The company wants to remove as few flights as possible so that the graph becomes disconnected. Determine the minimal number of flights to remove for:
a) $n = 2008$;
b) $n = 2007$.

positive_text:
Let $D(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{D(n)}{D(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n^2$ has no zero in its decimal representation;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{D(n^2)}{D(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n$ has no zero in its decimal representation.

negatives.text:
Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n^2$ contains only the digit zero;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n$ contains only the digit zero.

Let $P(n)$ denote the product of digits of the non-negative integer $n$. Prove that the sets $A$ and $B$ are unbounded, where:
a. $A = \left\{ \frac{P(n)}{P(n^2)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n^2$ contains no digit zero;
b. $B = \left\{ \frac{P(n^2)^2}{P(n)} \right\}$, where $n$ is a non-negative integer such that $n$ contains no digit zero.

positive_text:
Given a circle with $N$ points, Andriy and Olesya alternately draw chords between any two points, avoiding intersections with previously drawn chords. The player who creates a triangle using the drawn chords on their turn wins. Who wins the game when:
a) $N=14$;
b) $N=15$?

negatives.text:
Given $N$ points on a circle, Andriy and Olesya take turns drawing chords between any two points, avoiding intersections with previously drawn chords. The player who draws the last possible chord wins. Who wins if:
a) $N=14$;
b) $N=15$?

On a circle with $N$ points, players alternately draw chords between any two points, avoiding intersections. The player who completes a square using the drawn chords wins. Who wins if:
a) $N=14$;
b) $N=15$?

Let there be $N$ points on a circle. Andriy and Olesya take turns drawing chords between any two points, ensuring they do not intersect existing chords. The player who cannot move loses. Who wins if:
a) $N=14$;
b) $N=15$?

positive_text:
An $n \times n$ grid is divided into $n^2$ unit squares, with $n^2$ tokens initially placed in some configuration. In each move, a player can transfer one token from cell $A$ to cell $B$ and one token from cell $A$ to cell $C$, if cell $A$ has at least two tokens, and $B$ and $C$ are symmetric and adjacent to $A$ with respect to $A$. Is it possible to reach a state where each cell contains exactly one token, for the following cases:
a) $n = 2016$;
b) $n = 2017$?

negatives.text:
A square $n \times n$ is divided into $n^2$ cells, with $n^2$ tokens initially placed arbitrarily. In one move, a player can move one token from a cell $A$ to a cell $B$ and one token from $A$ to a cell $C$, provided $A$ has at least two tokens and $B$ and $C$ are symmetric and adjacent to $A$ with respect to $A$. Is it possible to reach a configuration where each cell has exactly one token, for:
a) $n = 2016$;
b) $n = 2018$?

A square $n \times n$ is divided into $n^2$ cells, with $n^2$ tokens initially placed arbitrarily. In one move, a player can move one token from a cell $A$ to a cell $B$ and one token from $A$ to a cell $C$, provided $A$ has at least two tokens and $B$ and $C$ are symmetric and adjacent to $A$ with respect to $A$. Is it possible to reach a configuration where each cell has exactly one token, for:
a) $n = 2015$;
b) $n = 2017$?

A square $n \times n$ is divided into $n^2$ cells, with $n^2$ tokens initially placed arbitrarily. In one move, a player can move one token from a cell $A$ to a cell $B$ and one token from $A$ to a cell $C$, provided $A$ has at least two tokens and $B$ and $C$ are symmetric and adjacent to $A$ with respect to $A$. Is it possible to reach a configuration where each cell has exactly one token, for:
a) $n = 2016$;
b) $n = 2017^2$?

positive_text:
Determine the maximum number $n$ such that there exists a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$ with the following properties:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime;
* for all $2 \le i \le n$, $a_i$ divides the product $a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17}$.

negatives.text:
What is the maximum length of a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$, if the following conditions hold:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for any $i: 2 \le i \le n$, $a_i \mid a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$ holds;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17} + 1$.

What is the maximum length of a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$, if the following conditions hold:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for any $i: 2 \le i \le n$, $a_i \mid a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$ holds;
* $a_n = 2^1 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5^3 \cdot 7^4 \cdot 11^5 \cdot 13^6 \cdot 17^7$.

What is the maximum length of a sequence of positive integers $a_1, a_2, \dots, a_n$, if the following conditions hold:
* $a_1 > 1$ is a prime number;
* for any $i: 2 \le i \le n$, $a_i$ is coprime to $a_1 a_2 \dots a_{i-1}$;
* $a_n = 2^2 \cdot 3^3 \cdot 5^5 \cdot 7^7 \cdot 11^{11} \cdot 13^{13} \cdot 17^{17}$.

positive_text:
Determine all positive integers $n$ such that an $n \times n$ square can be tiled (without overlap) using $1 \times k$ rectangles and a single $1 \times 1$ square tile, where:
a) $k=4$;
b) $k=8$.

negatives.text:
For which positive integers $n$, square $n \times n$ can be completely covered (without overlaps) by rectangles $k \times 1$ and one square $1 \times 1$, where:
a) $k=3$;
b) $k=7$?

For which positive integers $n$, square $n \times n$ can be completely covered (without overlaps) by rectangles $k \times 1$ and one square $2 \times 2$, where:
a) $k=4$;
b) $k=8$?

positive_text:
\text{Let } A = (a_{ij})\text{ be an } n \times n \text{ matrix. For all } x_i = \pm 1, \text{ the inequality}
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n} \left|\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{ij} x_j\right| \leq M$$
holds. Prove that $\sum_{i=1}^{n} |a_{ii}| \leq M$.

negatives.text:
\text{Given real numbers } a_{ij} \text{ for } 1 \leq i, j \leq n, \text{ if for all } x = (x_1, x_2, \ldots, x_n) \text{ with } x_j = 0 \text{ or } 1, \text{ we have}
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n} \left|\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{ij} x_j\right| \leq M,$$
then show that } \sum_{i=1}^{n} |a_{ii}| \leq M.

\text{Let } a_{ij} \in \mathbb{R} \text{ for } 1 \leq i, j \leq n. \text{ If the inequality}
$$\sum_{i=1}^{n} \left|\sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{ij} x_j\right|^2 \leq M$$
holds for all } x_j = \pm 1, \text{ prove that } \sum_{i=1}^{n} |a_{ii}| \leq M.

ext{Let } A, B 	ext{ be disjoint subsets of } \mathbb{N} 	ext{ such that } A \cup B = \mathbb{N}. 	ext{ A set } S 	ext{ is prohibited if for all } k, l \in A 	ext{ or } k, l \in B, 	ext{ it holds that } k + l \in S.

ext{a) Define Fibonacci numbers } f_{i} 	ext{ as } f_{1} = 1, f_{2} = 2, f_{i+1} = f_{i} + f_{i-1}. 	ext{ How many partitions are there such that } F = \{f_{1}, f_{2}, \dots\} 	ext{ is prohibited?}

ext{b) How many partitions are there where the set } P = \{2^{n} \mid n \in \mathbb{N}\} 	ext{ is prohibited? What if } P \subseteq A?

positive_text:
Let $T=\{\prod_{i=1}^{n} q_{i} \mid q_{1}, q_{2}, \ldots, q_{n}$ are distinct primes less than 30\}$. Assume $1$ is in $T$. Let $b_{1} \in T$. For each $n \geq 1$, define:
$$
\begin{gathered}
b_{n+1} = b_{n} \div (n+1) \quad \text{ if } (n+1) \mid b_{n} \\
b_{n+1} = (n+2) \cdot b_{n} \quad \text{ otherwise }
\end{gathered}
$$
How many distinct values of $b_{1}$ are there such that $b_{j} = b_{1}$ for infinitely many $j$?

negatives.text:
Let $S=\{p_{1} p_{2} \cdots p_{n} \mid p_{1}, p_{2}, \ldots, p_{n}$ are distinct primes and $p_{1}, \ldots, p_{n}<30\}$. Assume $1$ is in $S$. Let $a_{1}$ be an element of $S$. Define:
$$
\begin{cases}
a_{n+1} = a_{n} / (n+1), & (n+1) \mid a_{n} \\
a_{n+1} = (n+1) \cdot a_{n}, & (n+1) \nmid a_{n}
\end{cases}
$$
How many distinct possible values of $a_{1}$ are there such that $a_{j}=a_{1}$ for infinitely many $j$'s?

Let $S=\{p_{1} p_{2} \cdots p_{n} \mid p_{1}, p_{2}, \ldots, p_{n}$ are distinct primes and $p_{1}, \ldots, p_{n}<30\}$. Assume $1$ is in $S$. Let $a_{1}$ be an element of $S$. Define:
$$
\begin{cases}
a_{n+1} = a_{n} / (n+1), & (n+1) \mid a_{n} \\
a_{n+1} = (n+2) / a_{n}, & (n+1) \nmid a_{n}
\end{cases}
$$
How many distinct possible values of $a_{1}$ are there such that $a_{j}=a_{1}$ for infinitely many $j$'s?

Let $S=\{p_{1} p_{2} \cdots p_{n} \mid p_{1}, p_{2}, \ldots, p_{n}$ are distinct primes and $p_{1}, \ldots, p_{n} \leq 30\}$. Assume $1$ is in $S$. Let $a_{1}$ be an element of $S$. Define:
$$
\begin{cases}
a_{n+1} = a_{n} / (n+1), & (n+1) \mid a_{n} \\
a_{n+1} = (n+2) \cdot a_{n}, & (n+1) \nmid a_{n}
\end{cases}
$$
How many distinct possible values of $a_{1}$ are there such that $a_{j}=a_{1}$ for infinitely many $j$'s?

positive_text:
