Let two circles $\omega_{1}$ and $\omega_{2}$ be externally tangent at point $T$. A line intersects $\omega_{1}$ at $A$ and $B$, and is tangent to $\omega_{2}$ at $X$. The line $X T$ intersects $\omega_{1}$ again at $S$. Let $C$ lie on the arc $\overparen{T S}$ of $\omega_{1}$ that does not contain $A$ and $B$. Draw the tangent from $C$ to $\omega_{2}$, meeting $\omega_{2}$ at $Y$, ensuring that $C Y$ and $S T$ do not intersect. Let $I$ be the intersection of $X Y$ and $S C$. Prove:

a) $C, T, Y, I$ lie on a circle;

b) $I$ is the center of the excircle of $\triangle A B C$ opposite side $B C$.

negatives.text:
Two circles $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are externally tangent at point $T$. A line meets $k_{1}$ at $A$ and $B$ and is tangent to $k_{2}$ at point $X$. The line $X T$ meets $k_{1}$ at point $S$ and let $C$ be a point on the arc $\overparen{T S}$ which does not contain $A$ and $B$. Let $C Y$ be the tangent line to $k_{2}$ ($Y \in k_{2}$) such that the segments $C Y$ and $S T$ do not intersect. If $I$ is the intersection point of the lines $X Y$ and $S C$, prove that:

a) the points $C, T, Y$ and $I$ are concyclic;

b) $I$ is the center of the incircle of $\triangle A B C$.

Two circles $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are internally tangent at point $T$. A line meets $k_{1}$ at points $A$ and $B$ and is tangent to $k_{2}$ at point $X$. The line $X T$ meets $k_{1}$ at point $S$ and let $C$ be a point on the arc $\overparen{T S}$ which does not contain $A$ and $B$. Let $C Y$ be the tangent line to $k_{2}$ ($Y \in k_{2}$) such that the segments $C Y$ and $S T$ do not intersect. If $I$ is the intersection point of the lines $X Y$ and $S C$, prove that:

a) the points $C, T, Y$ and $I$ are concyclic;

b) $I$ is the center of the excircle of $\triangle A B C$ tangent to the side $B C$.

Two circles $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are externally tangent at point $T$. A line meets $k_{1}$ at points $A$ and $B$ and is tangent to $k_{2}$ at point $X$. The line $X T$ meets $k_{1}$ at point $S$ and let $C$ be a point on the arc $\overparen{T S}$ which does not contain $A$ and $B$. Let $C Y$ be the tangent line to $k_{2}$ ($Y \in k_{2}$) such that the segments $C Y$ and $S T$ intersect. If $I$ is the intersection point of the lines $X Y$ and $S C$, prove that:

a) the points $C, T, Y$ and $I$ are concyclic;

b) $I$ is the center of the excircle of $\triangle A B C$ tangent to the side $B C$.

positive_text:
Find all positive real numbers $a$ for which there exists a positive integer $n$ and disjoint, infinite subsets $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n$ of the set of integers such that:
(1) $\bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i = \mathbb{Z}$;
(2) for each $i$, any two elements in $A_i$ have a difference of at least $a^i$.

negatives.text:
Find all positive real numbers $a$ such that there exists a positive integer $n$ and sets $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n$ satisfying:
(1) each $A_i$ is infinite;
(2) $A_i \cap A_j = \emptyset$ for all $i \ne j$;
(3) $\bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i = \mathbb{Z}$;
(4) for every set $A_i$, the positive difference of any pair of elements in $A_i$ is at most $a^i$.

Find all positive real numbers $a$ such that there exists a positive integer $n$ and sets $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n$ satisfying:
(1) each $A_i$ is infinite;
(2) $A_i \cap A_j = \emptyset$ for all $i \ne j$;
(3) $\bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i = \mathbb{Z}$;
(4) for every set $A_i$, the positive difference of any pair of elements in $A_i$ is at least $a$.

Find all positive real numbers $a$ such that there exists a positive integer $n$ and sets $A_1, A_2, \dots, A_n$ satisfying:
(1) each $A_i$ is infinite;
(2) $A_i \cap A_j = \emptyset$ for all $i \ne j$;
(3) $\bigcup_{i=1}^n A_i = \mathbb{Z}$;
(4) for every set $A_i$, the sum of any pair of elements in $A_i$ is at least $a^i$.

positive_text:
Let $n$ be a positive integer. Determine all nonnegative real solutions $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$ to the system
$$
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{k=1}^{n} x_k^k &= n, \\
\sum_{k=1}^{n} kx_k &= \frac{n(n+1)}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Montrer qu'il existe une unique coloration des rationnels positifs en rouge et bleu telle que :\begin{itemize}
    \item $x$ et $x+1$ ont des couleurs opposées ;
    \item $x$ et $\frac{1}{x}$ partagent la même couleur ;
    \item $1$ est rouge.
\end{itemize}

negatives.text:
Prouver qu'il existe une unique manière de colorier chaque rationnel positif en rouge ou bleu telle que :\begin{itemize}
    \item $x$ et $x+1$ soient de la même couleur ;
    \item $x$ et $\frac{1}{x}$ soient de couleurs différentes ;
    \item $1$ soit rouge.
\end{itemize}

Prouver qu'il existe une unique manière de colorier chaque rationnel positif en rouge ou bleu telle que :\begin{itemize}
    \item $x$ et $x+1$ soient de couleurs différentes ;
    \item $x$ et $\frac{1}{x}$ soient de la même couleur ;
    \item $1$ soit bleu.
\end{itemize}

Prouver qu'il existe une unique manière de colorier chaque rationnel positif en rouge ou bleu telle que :\begin{itemize}
    \item $x$ et $x+1$ soient de couleurs différentes ;
    \item $x$ et $x^2$ soient de la même couleur ;
    \item $1$ soit rouge.
\end{itemize}

