Prove that for a triangle that is not equilateral, the following conditions are logically equivalent:
(a) the triangle's internal angles form an arithmetic sequence;
(b) the Euler line is parallel to the common tangent shared by the nine-point circle and the incircle.

negatives.text:
In a non-equilateral triangle, show that the following are equivalent:
(a) the angles of the triangle are in arithmetic progression;
(b) the common tangent to the nine-point circle and the in-circle is perpendicular to the Euler line.

In a non-equilateral triangle, show that the following are equivalent:
(a) the angles of the triangle are in geometric progression;
(b) the common tangent to the nine-point circle and the in-circle is parallel to the Euler line.

