positive_text:
Does there exist a number starting with digit $2$ such that, upon shifting the $2$ to the last position, the resulting number is
(a) twice the original;
(b) three times the original?

negatives.text:
Determine whether there exists a number that begins with $2$ having the property that, when the $2$ is moved to the end, the number is
(a) halved;
(b) tripled.

Determine whether there exists a number that begins with $2$ having the property that, when the $2$ is moved to the beginning of a second digit, the number is
(a) doubled;
(b) tripled.

Determine whether there exists a number that begins with $2$ having the property that, when the $2$ is moved to the end, the number is
(a) doubled;
(b) quadrupled.

positive_text:
Does there exist a function $g(m, n)$ from the positive integers to the positive integers such that
- $g(m, n)$ is a polynomial in $n$ for each fixed $m$;
- $g(m, n)$ is a polynomial in $m$ for each fixed $n$;
- Yet $g(m, n)$ is not expressible as a polynomial in $m$ and $n$ jointly?

negatives.text:
Is there a function $f(x, y)$ of two positive integers, taking positive integer values, such that
- For each fixed $x$, $f(x, y)$ is a polynomial function of $y$;
- For each fixed $y$, $f(x, y)$ is a polynomial function of $x$;
- And $f(x, y)$ is equal to a polynomial function of $x$ and $y$?

Determine whether there is a function $f(x, y)$ of two positive integers, taking positive integer values, such that
- For each fixed $x$, $f(x, y)$ is a polynomial function of $y$;
- For each fixed $y$, $f(x, y)$ is a polynomial function of $x$;
- However, $f(x, y)$ does not equal any rational function of $x$ and $y$.

Does there exist a function $f(x, y)$ of two positive integers, taking positive integer values, such that
- For each fixed $x$, $f(x, y)$ is a polynomial function of $y$;
- For each fixed $y$, $f(x, y)$ is a polynomial function of $x$;
- And $f(x, y)$ is not a polynomial function of $x$ or $y$ individually?

positive_text:
Can we assign each rational number a color (red or blue) so that the following are true for all rational $x$:
(i) $x$ is red if and only if $-x$ is blue, for $x \neq 0$;
(ii) $x$ is red if and only if $1-x$ is blue, for $x \neq 1 / 2$;
(iii) $x$ is red if and only if $1 / x$ is blue, for $x \neq 0, \pm 1$?

negatives.text:
Determine if it is possible to color each of the rational numbers either red or blue such that the following three conditions are all satisfied:
(i) $x$ and $-x$ are the same color, for all rational $x \neq 0$;
(ii) $x$ and $1-x$ are opposite colors, for all rational $x \neq 1 / 2$;
(iii) $x$ and $1 / x$ are opposite colors, for all rational $x \neq 0, \pm 1$.

Determine if it is possible to color each of the rational numbers either red or blue such that the following three conditions are all satisfied:
(i) $x$ and $-x$ are opposite colors, for all rational $x \neq 0$;
(ii) $x$ and $1+x$ are opposite colors, for all rational $x \neq -1 / 2$;
(iii) $x$ and $1 / x$ are opposite colors, for all rational $x \neq 0, \pm 1$.

Determine if it is possible to color each of the rational numbers either red or blue such that the following three conditions are all satisfied:
(i) $x$ and $-x$ are opposite colors, for all rational $x \neq 0$;
(ii) $x$ and $1-x$ are opposite colors, for all rational $x \neq 1 / 2$;
(iii) $x$ and $1 / x$ are the same color, for all rational $x \neq 0, \pm 1$.
